{
  "evidence": "TEXT_F: Budget Responsibility Lock with OBR verification; yearly deficit cuts and falling debt. Tough action on tax avoidance with a fairer tax mix (restore 50p rate; no rises in basic/higher IT/NI/VAT). Major NHS investment (20,000 nurses, 8,000 GPs), mental health parity, and support for carers. Counter‑extremism, protect neighbourhood policing, stricter border controls and benefit waits, English requirement for public‑facing roles. Uphold NATO and maintain a minimum credible nuclear deterrent; strengthen cyber security. Promote apprenticeships and raise the minimum wage; rebalance corporate governance for long‑termism. Decarbonisation target and green jobs. TEXT_C: Replace austerity with spending increases; raise minimum wage; expand apprenticeships; protect disability support; scrap the Bedroom Tax; overhaul sanctions. Oppose Trident renewal and new nuclear. Oppose mass surveillance; scrap the House of Lords; adopt STV. Make Scotland’s voice heard; never put Tories into power; extensive Home Rule and devolution. Keep UK in the EU with a double‑majority rule; exempt NHS/Scottish Water from TTIP. Climate action with fracking moratorium and community energy. Ensure the better‑off contribute more; end non‑doms; tackle avoidance.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts strongly invoke Care, but the electorate also places notable weight on Loyalty, Authority, and Proportionality. TEXT_F pairs social support with fiscal discipline, national‑security continuity (NATO, nuclear deterrent), controlled immigration, and rule‑bound governance, whereas TEXT_C’s Scotland‑first framing, anti‑Trident stance, and institutional disruption (scrapping the Lords, opposing surveillance) cut against those middling Authority/Loyalty/Proportionality priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
